+| — | #2166 / #2181 / #2193 (guard lane) | — | `bridge:queued` | 0 post-merge | — | #2203 cycle-17 rows (independent agent, Job 479095) |
+
+Sub-class A (rows 1-5 + guard 3): label stuck at `bridge:queued` after landing —
+the PR-merge → WO-plane propagation gap the guard already called variant-B.
+Sub-class B (rows 6-7): label propagation works, verdict field still never written —
+so the verdict writer is absent even where labels work. Both sub-classes share one
+observable: **no verdict is ever written, under any label state.**
+
+## 4. Impact
+
+1. **Duplicated verification cost.** Every agent must raw-fetch and content-diff every
+   landing claim. With N agents × M WOs this is the single largest recurring tax on
+   the bridge; a working verdict field would collapse it to one API read.
+2. **No tie-breaker for a lying label plane.** #2275/R58 documented an 18-WO
+   FALSE_REJECTION cohort (`bridge:rejected` labels on WOs whose code landed on main).
+   Labels have been observed wrong in both directions (stale-queued and false-
+   rejected); the verdict field is the one channel designed to arbitrate, and it is empty.
+3. **Reconciliation spend is growing.** #2334's pass-22/23 radar entries and the
+   guard's variant-B regression rows are manual bookkeeping that exists to compensate
+   for this plane. The cost is already visible in landed history.
+
+## 5. Why an agent-side fix is not possible
+
+1. Verdict fields are tower-internal state; no zero-credential tool writes them
+   (`bridge_submit_patch` creates WOs; no tool mutates feedback records).
+2. Labels are not agent-writable (no label-write tool on the 151-tool face; the
+   pick-bait report's Option A already noted the same absence for the pipeline array).
+3. Hand-editing landed WOs via new PRs would violate the append-only landed-history
+   doctrine and add a second SSOT — rejected per guard-3/7/8 discipline.
+
+## 6. Remediation options (owner / tower lane)
+
+- **Option A (recommended, read-side, no write-path risk):** `bridge_read_feedback`
+  derives a fallback verdict from the label/state it already reads —
+  `bridge:landed` + closed → `APPLIED_LABEL_DERIVED`, with a new
+  `verdictSource: "label-fallback"` field and the raw null preserved as
+  `intakeVerdict: null`. Unblocks machine-readability immediately; #2202-style
+  tower-side deploy.
+- **Option B (root fix, HITL deploy lane):** the submission processor that assigns
+  labels also writes the intake verdict record at gate-decision time. Fixes the root
+  for both sub-classes; queues behind the #2225/#2226 platform deploy lane.
+- **Option C (honest-contract):** if verdicts are architecturally impossible for
+  anonymous-bridge WOs, amend the `feedbackTool` contract string to stop promising
+  them and canonize raw-blob-comparison as the official verification doctrine.
+  Honest, but permanently retains the duplication tax.
+
